    thinking: "**Analyzing Initial Structure**\n\nI\'ve observed that `App.tsx` is currently quite minimal. My attention has shifted to examining the features of www.prepara.cv, specifically as indicated by the URL\'s content. I\'m focusing on the service\'s core value proposition: assisting users with their CVs.\n\n\n**Developing Key Features**\n\nNow, I\'m concentrating on the core offerings. I\'m thinking about the \"aligned CV\" concept and the supporting career coaching. I\'ve broken it down into feature areas: networking tools, cover letters, and interview simulators. A four-step process guides it. I\'m envisioning the portal and focusing on Curriculum/Profile editors and ATS optimizers with customizable inputs.\n\n\n**Exploring API Requirements**\n\nI\'m now diving into feature implementation. My focus is on ensuring the server-side infrastructure can support the new feature additions. Specifically, I\'m verifying the API\'s readiness for the cover letter generator, intelligent networking templates, the interactive interview simulator, and company dossier features. I\'m checking the existing endpoints.\n\n\n**Confirming Backend Readiness**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that the backend already offers endpoints, as detailed in `server.ts`. These include endpoints for CV optimization, cover letter generation, networking templates, and interview simulation. Furthermore, the company dossier feature seems to be grounded via Google Search. This is excellent news, as I can start building the frontend components within `src/components/`, integrating them within `src/App.tsx`.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I\'m going to create `src/components/NetworkingGenerator.tsx` to implement the \"Networking Inteligente\" feature. This feature will allow the user to type in a target company and a cargo (job role), and get 2 ready-to-use professional networking templates (with a mock LinkedIn message preview and email template, along with coaching tips on how to find the specific contact person)."
