  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Understanding Variable Scope**\n\nI\'ve hit a snag in Express routes: variables destructured within a `try` block using `const` are only accessible inside that block. My `catch` block can\'t see `cvData`, `jobDescription`, and the others I declared that way. This is crucial for error handling in the routes.\n\n\n**Resolving Scope Issues**\n\nI\'ve got it! Moving the variable destructuring outside the `try` block is the key, making them accessible in both `try` and `catch` blocks. The `/api/optimize-cv` and `/api/generate-cover-letter` endpoints are fixed. I\'m decluttering the code and eliminating those pesky linting errors in one fell swoop. Now I\'ll address the remaining endpoints.\n\n\n**Refactoring Route Handlers**\n\nI\'m systematically addressing the remaining routes. For `/api/generate-networking`, `/api/interview-simulator`, `/api/interview-evaluate`, and `/api/company-dossier`, I\'m moving the necessary variables before the `try` block, to broaden their scope. I am verifying the exact variable names and parameters by reviewing the code to ensure precise replacement. This thorough approach should eliminate any remaining scope issues and improve error handling across the board.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will modify `/server.ts` to destructure the request body properties *before* the `try` blocks in each route, so they are available within both the `try` and `catch` scopes of the handler function."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
      nanos: 711969020
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
